// bubbler profile: claude-code
//
// The native installer puts a symlink at `~/.local/bin/claude` into
// `~/.local/share/claude/versions/`; npm global puts `/usr/bin/claude`.
// Both resolve through the sandbox PATH (`/usr/bin` first); whichever
// is present on the host is used, and `bubbler try --profile claude-code
// --explain` shows which shares bound or `absent on this host, skipped`.
// Updating happens on the host; inside, DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER stops the
// attempt against a read-only tree.
//
// Create the instance once:
//     bubbler create claude-code --profile claude-code
// then, for every run, name the project and share it:
//     cd <project>
//     bubbler run claude-code --share .
// The share is for that run only, and the sandbox starts in it.
//
// The private home holds its own `~/.claude`: log in once inside
// (`/login`, copy the URL into a host browser, paste the code back), or
// copy `~/.claude/.credentials.json` into the instance home yourself.
// The host's `~/.claude` — history, settings, credentials — is never
// visible.
//
// Deliberately absent: an ssh agent, a keyring, the host's dotfiles, a
// display. `git` over SSH needs a key in the private home. The editor
// bridge (`/ide`) listens on the host's loopback and does not cross the
// network namespace.
//
// `userns "disable"`: bubbler is the boundary. Claude Code's own
// bubblewrap sandbox warns and is skipped; a Playwright or Chromium MCP
// server needs `userns "allow"` instead.
//
// Other switches, as `env` nodes: DISABLE_TELEMETRY="1",
// CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC="1".
//
// Stricter: `claude-code-strict` is this profile with the egress
// filtered by name — the tool reaches the hosts Anthropic documents as
// its network access requirements and nothing else, through a CONNECT
// proxy bubbler runs in the sandbox's network namespace. Create an
// instance from that profile, or paste the block below over the bare
// `network` node here; the two are the same text, and a test holds them
// together.
//
//     lint-allow "outbound-deny" reason="the deny is the point of this profile: egress is filtered by name, through the proxy, rather than by address"
//     network {
//         outbound "deny"
//         // the API, the sign-in pages, and the OAuth exchange a login code goes
//         // through; the API also answers WebFetch's domain safety check, and
//         // claude.com is a target WebFetch is pre-approved for
//         allow-host "api.anthropic.com"
//         allow-host "claude.ai"
//         allow-host "claude.com"
//         allow-host "platform.claude.com"
//         // claude.ai connectors (ENABLE_CLAUDEAI_MCP_SERVERS=false drops this)
//         allow-host "mcp-proxy.anthropic.com"
//         // releases and version checks, plugin executables, plugin metadata, and
//         // the npm packages an `npx`-launched MCP server installs. The Google
//         // storage host is shared hosting anyone may publish to, so listing it
//         // names a host rather than a party: anything in the sandbox that reaches
//         // it can put bytes in a bucket of its own. Drop that line if no plugin
//         // here needs its metadata.
//         allow-host "downloads.claude.ai"
//         allow-host "storage.googleapis.com"
//         allow-host "registry.npmjs.org"
//         // Claude in Chrome, and Artifacts (CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_ARTIFACT=1
//         // drops the second)
//         allow-host "bridge.claudeusercontent.com"
//         allow-host "*.frame.claudeusercontent.com"
//         // the changelog `/release-notes` reads. Shared hosting as well — every
//         // public repository on GitHub is under this one name — so it is a way
//         // out as much as a source; drop it if the changelog can go unread.
//         allow-host "raw.githubusercontent.com"
//         // telemetry and error reports, on Datadog's us5 site
//         allow-host "http-intake.logs.us5.datadoghq.com"
//         allow-host "browser-intake-us5-datadoghq.com"
//         // the documentation the tool looks things up in
//         allow-host "code.claude.com"
//     }
//
// Delete a group and the tool loses what its comment names. `WebFetch`
// of a URL off the list, an MCP server reaching a service of its own,
// and `git` or `curl` against an unnamed forge stop working whatever
// you keep; each is one more `allow-host` line. The application has no
// DNS of its own under this node, so anything in there that ignores
// `HTTPS_PROXY` fails at the name lookup rather than at the connection,
// and the run needs a delegated cgroup2 subtree (a systemd user session
// has one) beside the `passt` and `nftables` packages.
network
userns "disable"
env DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER="1"
home-share {
    ".local/bin/claude" mode=ro optional=#true
    ".local/share/claude" mode=ro optional=#true
}
command "claude"
